11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L18",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 6, 1862, about 5 p.m. through the night of April 6–7","place":"Grant’s final line above Pittsburg Landing, Dill Branch, the Tennessee River, and the captured United States camps at Shiloh, Tennessee","result":"Chalmers’s and Jackson’s brigades attacked the United States left at about 6 p.m. Artillery on Grant’s ridge, supporting infantry, the gunboats Tyler and Lexington, and the flooded Dill Branch stopped the two-brigade attack. Other Confederate commanders were assembling a larger assault when P.G.T. Beauregard ordered the army to break contact near sundown and return to the captured camps. Beauregard incorrectly believed Grant’s army could be finished the next morning and that Buell remained distant. The 36th Indiana had already crossed at Pittsburg Landing, more Army of the Ohio troops arrived during the night, and Lew Wallace’s division approached from the north. Confederate units remained dispersed and poorly reorganized. Grant rejected retreat and ordered an attack at daylight","facts":[{"label":"Final attack began","value":"About 6 p.m., roughly 20 minutes before sunset"},{"label":"Confederate attacking force","value":"Chalmers’s and Jackson’s brigades without a coordinated supporting assault"},{"label":"First Buell regiment","value":"36th Indiana, ferried across after reaching the east bank about 5 p.m."},{"label":"Men below the bluff","value":"Podcast estimate: 10,000 to 15,000 stragglers and disorganized soldiers"},{"label":"Attack stopped","value":"Artillery, infantry, naval fire, and difficult ground at Dill Branch"},{"label":"Beauregard’s order","value":"Break contact near sundown and bivouac in the captured camps"},{"label":"Night gunboat fire","value":"One shell from each gunboat about every 15 minutes"},{"label":"Grant’s decision","value":"Remain west of the Tennessee River and counterattack at daylight"}],"maps":[{"title":"Shiloh battlefield today","caption":"The NPS map locates the ridge above Pittsburg Landing, Dill Branch, Crump’s Landing, the river crossings, and the captured camps.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"The final United States line","caption":"Webster’s massed artillery anchored the center. Hurlbut supported the guns, McClernand and Sherman held farther west and north, and the timberclads covered Dill Branch.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/shiloh-auto-tour-introduction-grant-s-last-line-stop-1.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-grants-last-line.svg","alt":"Map of Grant’s final defensive line at Shiloh"},{"title":"Nightfall and incoming reinforcements","caption":"Chalmers and Jackson attacked across Dill Branch as Nelson’s lead regiment crossed the river. Beauregard then stopped the Confederate advance; Lew Wallace and additional Army of the Ohio troops arrived after dark.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nightfall-reinforcements.svg","alt":"Map of the final attack, the overnight positions, and United States reinforcement routes"},{"title":"Lew Wallace’s approach","caption":"Wallace’s 3rd Division reached Grant from the north after a march from Crump’s Landing by way of the Shunpike and River Road.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/people/lew-wallace.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-lew-wallace-march.svg","alt":"Map of Lew Wallace’s march to Shiloh"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"The 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the battlefield roads, ravines, camps, and the ground separating the armies at nightfall.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of the Battle of Shiloh"}],"moments":[{"time":"0:39","audioTime":39,"title":"Chalmers and Jackson began the final attack","detail":"At about 6 p.m., the two brigades advanced toward the United States left with sunset about twenty minutes away."},{"time":"1:07","audioTime":67,"title":"Grant’s line opened fire","detail":"Webster’s artillery, supporting infantry, and the two timberclads fired into the Confederate advance."},{"time":"2:28","audioTime":148,"title":"Buell’s first regiment arrived","detail":"Colonel Jacob Ammen’s 36th Indiana crossed the Tennessee River with division commander William Nelson after reaching the east bank at about 5 p.m."},{"time":"3:13","audioTime":193,"title":"The regiment pushed through the landing area","detail":"Thousands of disorganized soldiers crowded below the bluff. Nelson cleared space so the regiment could land and move uphill."},{"time":"6:54","audioTime":414,"title":"Lew Wallace heard the final-line guns","detail":"His 3rd Division was about half an hour away when officers recognized the siege guns and timberclads firing near Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"7:39","audioTime":459,"title":"The two-brigade attack failed","detail":"Chalmers and Jackson crossed the most difficult ground on the front and received concentrated fire without enough support. Both brigades withdrew."},{"time":"8:19","audioTime":499,"title":"Other Confederate commanders prepared a larger attack","detail":"Bragg, Polk, and Withers were moving additional troops forward when units began receiving an order to stop."},{"time":"10:02","audioTime":602,"title":"Beauregard ordered the army to break contact","detail":"Near sundown, he directed units to withdraw to a line near the captured United States camps."},{"time":"10:30","audioTime":630,"title":"The order rested on incorrect information","detail":"Beauregard believed Grant was defeated, Buell was still a day or two away, and gunboat fire was causing more damage than it actually was."},{"time":"13:03","audioTime":783,"title":"A successful additional assault was uncertain","detail":"The podcast concludes that darkness, Confederate exhaustion and disorganization, and the strength of Grant’s position made a breakthrough unlikely."},{"time":"14:14","audioTime":854,"title":"Cold rain began after the fighting stopped","detail":"Thousands of wounded men remained between the lines while a heavy rain continued through much of the night."},{"time":"15:27","audioTime":927,"title":"The gunboats shelled through the night","detail":"Tyler and Lexington alternated shots about every fifteen minutes. Some shells landed near wounded soldiers as well as Confederate positions."},{"time":"17:10","audioTime":1030,"title":"The Confederate army remained disorganized","detail":"Many soldiers stayed in captured camps rather than rejoining their units. Beauregard’s overnight reorganization was incomplete."},{"time":"17:59","audioTime":1079,"title":"Forrest reported United States reinforcements","detail":"Near midnight, Forrest told Chalmers that troops were crossing the river continuously, but he could not locate Beauregard or most senior commanders."},{"time":"18:42","audioTime":1122,"title":"United States officers considered withdrawal","detail":"James McPherson reported that Grant’s army was badly damaged and asked whether he should prepare a retreat."},{"time":"19:21","audioTime":1161,"title":"Grant ordered a counterattack","detail":"Grant rejected retreat and stated that the army would attack at daylight on April 7."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Held the final ridge and ordered a counterattack for daylight"},{"commander":"Joseph D. Webster","command":"Chief of staff and artillery coordinator","subordinates":"Directed the massed guns that stopped the final Confederate advance"},{"commander":"Don Carlos Buell","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Reinforcements arriving from the east bank of the Tennessee River"},{"commander":"William Nelson / Jacob Ammen","command":"4th Division / 10th Brigade, Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"36th Indiana was the first regiment ferried to the battlefield"},{"commander":"Lew Wallace","command":"3rd Division, Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Approached the United States right from Crump’s Landing after dark"},{"commander":"William Gwin / James W. Shirk","command":"USS Tyler / USS Lexington","subordinates":"Fired on the final attack and shelled Confederate positions through the night"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"P.G.T. Beauregard","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Ordered the attack stopped and attempted an overnight reorganization"},{"commander":"Braxton Bragg / Leonidas Polk / Jones M. Withers","command":"Late-afternoon field direction","subordinates":"Were assembling additional troops when Beauregard’s halt order arrived"},{"commander":"James R. Chalmers / John K. Jackson","command":"Brigades on the Confederate right","subordinates":"Made the unsupported assault across Dill Branch at about 6 p.m."},{"commander":"Nathan Bedford Forrest","command":"Cavalry","subordinates":"Observed the overnight United States reinforcement movement and tried to report it"}]}],"people":[{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Held the army west of the river and decided to counterattack on April 7"},{"name":"P.G.T. Beauregard","role":"Stopped the Confederate attack near sundown based partly on incorrect reports about Buell and Grant","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William Nelson","role":"Led the first Army of the Ohio troops across at Pittsburg Landing","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jacob Ammen","role":"Commanded the 36th Indiana, the first regiment from Buell’s army to cross the river","side":"United States"},{"name":"James R. Chalmers","role":"Co-led the final Confederate attack and later received Forrest’s reinforcement report","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John K. Jackson","role":"His brigade attacked beside Chalmers across Dill Branch","side":"Confederacy"},{"personId":"nathan-bedford-forrest","role":"Reported that United States reinforcements were crossing throughout the night"},{"name":"James B. McPherson","role":"Reported the army’s condition and asked Grant whether to prepare a retreat","side":"United States"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Tried to assemble a larger final assault before receiving Beauregard’s halt order","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/braxton-bragg.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670042/"},{"name":"William T. Sherman","role":"Commanded the United States right and met Grant during the rainy night","side":"United States","image":"/images/william-t-sherman.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670507/"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing in 1862","caption":"The 36th Indiana and later Army of the Ohio regiments crossed here while wounded and disorganized troops crowded the riverbank.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"Wounded soldiers remained across the wet battlefield between the armies during the night of April 6–7.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battle-print.jpg","alt":"Historical print of the Battle of Shiloh","caption":"The first day ended with Grant still holding Pittsburg Landing and Confederate forces occupying the abandoned camps.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 123","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/123-shiloh-part-the-thirteenth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Grant’s Last Line","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/shiloh-auto-tour-introduction-grant-s-last-line-stop-1.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Grant’s Left Flank","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/grant-s-left-flank-tour-stop-21.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Lew Wallace","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/lew-wallace.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pittsburg Landing photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"}]}}]}]
